{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghWAhjaoCsfXLiHhicQb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For this tetrahedral Co(II) complex, **Dq = 433 cm⁻¹** and **B ≈ 878 cm⁻¹**. C cannot be found from these bands.\n\n**Approach.** In a tetrahedral field, d⁷ follows the same Tanabe–Sugano pattern as octahedral d³. The ground term is ⁴A₂(F), and the three spin-allowed bands are:\n\n- ν₁: ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂(F), with ν₁ = 10Dq\n- ν₂: ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(F), with ν₂ = 15Dq + 7.5B − ½√(225B² + 100Dq² − 180DqB)\n- ν₃: ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(P), with ν₃ = 15Dq + 7.5B + ½√(225B² + 100Dq² − 180DqB)\n\n**Assigning the bands.** The lowest band, in the near-IR, is ν₁ = 4 330 cm⁻¹. The other two are ν₂ = 7 520 cm⁻¹ and ν₃ = 18 640 cm⁻¹ (visible).\n\n**Finding Dq.** Since ν₁ = 10Dq, Dq = 4 330 / 10 = **433 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**Finding B.** Adding ν₂ and ν₃ cancels the square-root term:\n\n- ν₂ + ν₃ = 30Dq + 15B\n- 26 160 = 12 990 + 15B\n- B ≈ **878 cm⁻¹**\n\n**Checking the result.** Put Dq = 433 and B = 878 back into the formulas:\n\n- The square root is √(1.2377 × 10⁸) ≈ 11 125 cm⁻¹.\n- 15Dq + 7.5B = 6 495 + 6 585 = 13 080 cm⁻¹.\n- ν₂ = 13 080 − 5 563 ≈ 7 517 cm⁻¹ (observed 7 520).\n- ν₃ = 13 080 + 5 563 ≈ 18 643 cm⁻¹ (observed 18 640).\n\nThe calculated values match the observed bands, so the assignment holds together.\n\n**Nephelauxetic ratio.** Free-ion values for Co²⁺ B are quoted between about 970 and 1 120 cm⁻¹. Using 1 120 cm⁻¹ gives β = 878 / 1 120 ≈ 0.78, a moderate reduction from the free ion.\n\n**Why C is not given.** All three bands are quartet → quartet transitions, so their energies depend only on Dq and B. Finding C would need a spin-forbidden (doublet) band.\n\n{\"Dq\": 433, \"B\": 878, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq from nu1=10Dq and B from nu2+nu3=30Dq+15B; C is not determined by spin-allowed bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_cr